+| [Terraform plugin protocol](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/plugin/terraform-plugin-protocol) and [go-plugin](https://github.com/hashicorp/go-plugin) | Explicit protocol compatibility; go-plugin uses RPC with a reliable local-network assumption. | **Adapt:** version the actual host/contribution contract and reject incompatible negotiation. Compare an existing adapter before a new SDK. | Neither gRPC nor Go nor a local-process reliability model is selected. A remote Internet service needs its actual failure, identity and network contracts. |
+| [VS Code Webviews](https://code.visualstudio.com/api/extension-guides/webview) and [UX guidance](https://code.visualstudio.com/api/ux-guidelines/webviews) | Custom views have specific resource, message, sanitization/CSP, theming and accessibility obligations; custom UI is not always necessary. | **Adapt:** prefer adequate native presentation; qualify admitted custom UI boundaries and retained historical meaning after its renderer disappears. | Product-specific browser restrictions are not a complete Tesbo threat model; documented accessibility guidance is not observed human usability. |
+| [Forge app versions](https://developer.atlassian.com/platform/forge/versions/) and [storage lifecycle](https://developer.atlassian.com/platform/forge/storage-reference/hosted-storage-data-lifecycle/) | Installation/version and consent changes are distinct; retained uninstall data is not a generally readable archive or automatic recovery. | **Adapt:** evaluate material authority changes separately from package version labels; define lawful readable history, export/migration and recovery before admitting persistent custom formats. | Forge's retention periods, approval model and support process do not become Tesbo policy. No recovery success rate was measured here. |
+| [Current Grafana Frontend Sandbox status](https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/administration/plugin-management/plugin-frontend-sandbox/) | The public-preview feature is documented as deprecated and scheduled for removal in 13.4. | **Reject as a presently selected mechanism:** check lifecycle/maintenance before adopting an attractive precedent; retain only the relevant problem/constraint as research. | This does not reject all browser isolation designs or decide Tesbo's runtime. A dated source needs rechecking when adoption is considered. |
+
+### Decisions changed by this comparison
+
+The proposed v1.4.49 contract now distinguishes package dependencies, work-unit dependencies and application assurance relationships; it adds namespaced contribution conflicts, explicit provider selection/default binding, compatible state recovery, usable historical representation and profile-specific effects. It applies existing revocation, stopping, budgets and native evidence controls rather than reproducing them in a second plugin framework. Installation, connection, project enablement and actual grant remain separate, including the actor who can resolve each blocked state. The research has not selected a first profile, marketplace, SDK, executable/UI runtime or automatic update policy.
+
+**Stopping point for this design increment.** These sources support concrete proposed boundaries and falsifiable acceptance dimensions. Additional broad searching is unlikely to resolve the remaining product choices without a selected user outcome. Targeted deeper source/incident/version/license analysis resumes when a profile or dependency is considered for implementation, or when a relied-on assumption changes. Runtime controls and historical-readability cases remain NOT RUN. Existing functionality, configuration, a native adapter or no change remain valid alternatives to installing a plugin.
+
+**Reusable engineering lesson.** Investigate established outcomes and failures, separate the evidence from the inferred mechanism, identify applicability/counterexamples, and test the contextual decision through the existing owner. The detailed portable method belongs to canonical 06 and the existing engineering/team instructions. This research document supplies a worked application, not another binding team charter or an installed Skill.
